A1. Something Adam never had to contend with. ~ MOTHERINLAW
A11. It is proper for. ~ BEHOOVES
A12. What they did after their vacation. ~ OWED
A14. This is always in doubt. ~ DO
A15. What they did to keep the wolf from the door. ~ MOVED
A16. If you are going to make a garden this is a good place to raise a little sweet corn. ~ TOE
A17. This is hard to get on Sunday morning. ~ UP
A19. Where a big shot lodged. ~ INN
A21. The beginning of the end. ~ EN
A22. This one is superstitious about black cats. ~ RAT
A23. Possesses. ~ HAS
A24. Even a blind tiger can not do this. ~ SEE
A25. An extract from Ade. ~ LEMON
A27. A town in Sumatra noted for its tobacco plantations. ~ DELI
A28. Where fast drivers are sure to go. ~ AHEAD
A31. This one will make you think, paws and flea. ~ DOG
A32. A human thing to do. ~ ERR
A33. This is even more so than Ver. No. 6. ~ REDDER
A35. What traffic does to the pedestrian. ~ RUINS
A37. This must be right. (abbr.) ~ RT
A38. When we "tiptoe through the tulips." ~ MAY
A39. A winsome thing that is always getting in the papers. ~ CONTEST
A41. First this was dry, then it got into hot water and finally got drunk. ~ TEA
A42. A big sell. ~ AD
A43. This is also good to the last drop. ~ COCOA
A44. A system of weights and measures. ~ METRIC
A46. It takes a real artist to draw this (in Chicago). ~ PAY
A47. You will not find this in a single state. ~ MRS
A49. A thick-skinned creature that bores people to death (contr.). ~ RHINO
A50. Ask the editor how he starts editorials. ~ EDIT
A52. Something even money never does. ~ LASTS
A54. This belonged to Balaam. ~ ASS
A55. This helps to make nonsense for puzzle fans. ~ NON
A56. You might find a grave situation here. ~ SOD
A57. This will put your throat in good shape for the doctor. ~ AH
A58. A Hawaiian memory. ~ LEI
A59. Where you will come eventually. ~ TO
A60. This describes a prominent feature in one of Colleen Moore's pictures. ~ PUG
A61. What magnates do with wealth. ~ AMASS
A64. This stands for real service. ~ RS
A65. A perfume agent. ~ ROSE
A67. An unusual child. ~ OBEDIENT
A69. Because of lack of space we can not give much help here. ~ KITCHENETTE

D1. This ended the foursome. (Something personal.) ~ ME
D2. A unit of electrical resistance. ~ OHM
D3. This is more than enough. ~ TOO
D4. No matter how humble this might be a home. ~ HOVEL
D5. They have also done this to the hem line. ~ EVENED
D6. Believe it or not this came from the lips of a chorus girl. ~ RED
D7. This is what started Isidore. ~ IS
D8. A take-off on the cashier. ~ LOOT
D9. A holy terror. ~ AWE
D10. This means the firm. ~ WE
D11. This is a hard one and you will get nothing out of it. ~ BONEHEAD
D13. What a life to lead ! ~ DUAL
D14. An international scrap. ~ DISARMAMENT
D16. This must have been the "sunnyside up." ~ TAN
D18. After a long and hard spell this was diagnosed in a polyclinic. ~ PSITTACOSIS
D20. Broke. ~ NEEDY
D22. The kind of an elephant that roams alone. ~ ROGUE
D23. The kind of style a man admires. ~ HER
D26. What every mason knows. ~ MORTARS
D27. These are good for indigestion (abbr.). ~ DRS
D29. Same as Hor. No. 42. (It pays to advertise.) ~ AD
D30. Some poor goose is sure to be fooled with this one. ~ DECOY
D32. This is what he had to do in order to get into the club. ~ ENTER
D34. A fabulous bird. ~ ROC
D36. A doctrine (not a female doctor). ~ ISM
D37. What a puzzle fan will do after erasing. ~ REINSERT
D40. This will never stay put. ~ NOMAD
D41. We will make this one attempt. ~ TRIAL
D43. This always comes baek to the owner. ~ CAT
D45. A lisp. ~ TH
D46. If you get stuck here use safety first. ~ PIN
D48. A part of a flower. ~ STAMEN
D51. This always hinges on something else. ~ DOOR
D52. It is easy rolling off this. ~ LOG
D53. This is what Dad pulled the other night. ~ SHADE
D56. No diet can keep this from being fat. ~ SUET
D60. A Greek character. ~ PSI
D61. A high-powered Lincoln. ~ ABE
D62. What the Prince of Wales will do when he reaches the throne. ~ SIT
D63. This is often false. ~ SET
D66. The two most satisfactory letters in the alphabet. ~ OK
D67. The surprising part of Ohio. ~ OH
D68. The beginning of necessity and the end of fortune. ~ NE


